---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---

<Layout section="OAuth 2.0" title="OAuth 2.0 Fundamentals">
  <div class="page-wrap">
    <div class="learn-layout">
      <nav class="toc">
        <div class="toc-label">On this page</div>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="#problem">The Problem OAuth Solves</a></li>
          <li><a href="#what-is">What is OAuth 2.0?</a></li>
          <li><a href="#actors">The Four Actors</a></li>
          <li><a href="#api-keys-vs-oauth">API Keys vs OAuth Tokens</a></li>
          <li><a href="#scopes">Scopes &amp; User Consent</a></li>
          <li><a href="#clients">Public vs Confidential Clients</a></li>
          <li><a href="#client-trust">Client Trust &amp; App Models</a></li>
          <li><a href="#channels">Front Channel vs Back Channel</a></li>
          <li><a href="#framework-variance">Framework vs Protocol Variance</a></li>
          <li><a href="#endpoints">OAuth Endpoints</a></li>
          <li><a href="#security">Web Security 101 Checklist</a></li>
        </ul>
        <hr class="toc-divider" />
        <nav class="toc-nav">
          <a href={base + '/'}>Home</a>
          <a href={base + '/flows'}>Grant Types &rarr;</a>
        </nav>
      </nav>

      <main class="content">
        <div class="page-header">
          <span class="badge badge-beginner">Beginner &middot; 25 min</span>
          <h1>OAuth 2.0 Fundamentals</h1>
          <p class="lead">Understanding delegated authorization, the password anti-pattern, actor topologies, client trust models, token consumers, and modern security best practices under RFC 6749 and RFC 9700.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- PROBLEM -->
        <section id="problem">
          <h2>The Problem OAuth Solves: The Password Anti-Pattern</h2>
          <p>Before OAuth, if a third-party application (e.g., Yelp or a photo printing service) needed access to a user's data on another platform (e.g., Google Contacts or Flickr), the third-party app asked the user for their raw username and password.</p>

          <div style="background:var(--surface); border:1px solid #f87171; border-radius:var(--radius); padding:1.5rem; margin:1.5rem 0;">
            <div style="font-weight:700; color:#f87171; margin-bottom:0.75rem; font-size:1.05rem;">⛔ The Password Anti-Pattern (Legacy Approach)</div>
            <p style="margin-bottom:0.5rem; font-size:0.95rem;">"Enter your Google Email and Password so PhotoPrint can download your albums:"</p>
            <div style="background:#070c14; border:1px solid var(--border); padding:1rem; border-radius:4px; font-family:var(--font-mono); font-size:0.85rem; margin-bottom:1rem;">
              Username: user@gmail.com<br/>
              Password: &bull;&bull;&bull;&bull;&bull;&bull;&bull;&bull;&bull;&bull;&bull;&bull;
            </div>
            <ul style="margin:0; font-size:0.9rem; line-height:1.6;">
              <li><strong>Full Account Takeover:</strong> PhotoPrint gains unrestricted access to all your Gmail, Drive files, and Search history.</li>
              <li><strong>Cannot Revoke:</strong> To revoke PhotoPrint's access, you must change your master Google password, breaking all other apps.</li>
              <li><strong>Stored in Plaintext:</strong> Third-party apps stored user passwords in plaintext or reversible encryption to perform background syncing.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- WHAT IS -->
        <section id="what-is">
          <h2>What is OAuth 2.0? (RFC 6749)</h2>
          <blockquote>
            "The OAuth 2.0 authorization framework enables a third-party application to obtain limited access to an HTTP service, either on behalf of a resource owner by orchestrating an approval interaction between the resource owner and the HTTP service, or by allowing the third-party application to obtain access on its own behalf."
          </blockquote>

          <div style="background:var(--surface); border:1px solid var(--primary); border-radius:var(--radius); padding:1.5rem; margin:1.5rem 0;">
            <div style="font-weight:700; color:var(--primary); margin-bottom:0.75rem; font-size:1.05rem;">🚗 The Valet Key Analogy</div>
            <p style="margin-bottom:0.75rem;">When you give a <strong>valet key</strong> to a parking attendant:</p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Limited Access:</strong> The valet can start the engine and park the car, but cannot open the locked trunk or glovebox.</li>
              <li><strong>Short Duration:</strong> The valet key is used only while parking and returned immediately after.</li>
              <li><strong>No Master Key:</strong> You never hand over your master key or house keys on the keyring.</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="margin-top:0.75rem; margin-bottom:0;">In OAuth 2.0, the <strong>Access Token</strong> is the digital valet key: it grants scoped, temporary, revocable access to specific API endpoints without ever disclosing the user's master credentials.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ACTORS -->
        <section id="actors">
          <h2>The Four OAuth Actors (RFC 6749 Section 1.1)</h2>
          <p>Every OAuth 2.0 architecture revolves around 4 distinct roles:</p>
          
          <div style="display:grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr)); gap:1rem; margin:1.5rem 0;">
            <div style="background:var(--surface); border:1px solid var(--border); padding:1.25rem; border-radius:var(--radius);">
              <h3 style="color:var(--primary); margin-top:0; font-size:1rem;">1. Resource Owner</h3>
              <p style="font-size:0.88rem; color:var(--muted);"><strong>The User (e.g. Alice)</strong></p>
              <p style="font-size:0.88rem; margin-bottom:0;">The human entity that owns the data and has authority to grant access to it.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="background:var(--surface); border:1px solid var(--border); padding:1.25rem; border-radius:var(--radius);">
              <h3 style="color:var(--primary); margin-top:0; font-size:1rem;">2. Client</h3>
              <p style="font-size:0.88rem; color:var(--muted);"><strong>The Application (e.g. PhotoPrint App)</strong></p>
              <p style="font-size:0.88rem; margin-bottom:0;">The application requesting access to protected resources on behalf of the user.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="background:var(--surface); border:1px solid var(--border); padding:1.25rem; border-radius:var(--radius);">
              <h3 style="color:var(--primary); margin-top:0; font-size:1rem;">3. Authorization Server</h3>
              <p style="font-size:0.88rem; color:var(--muted);"><strong>The IdP (e.g. Okta, Entra ID, Google)</strong></p>
              <p style="font-size:0.88rem; margin-bottom:0;">Authenticates the Resource Owner, prompts for consent, and mints cryptographic tokens.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="background:var(--surface); border:1px solid var(--border); padding:1.25rem; border-radius:var(--radius);">
              <h3 style="color:var(--primary); margin-top:0; font-size:1rem;">4. Resource Server</h3>
              <p style="font-size:0.88rem; color:var(--muted);"><strong>The Protected API (e.g. Photo Storage API)</strong></p>
              <p style="font-size:0.88rem; margin-bottom:0;">Hosts protected resources and validates incoming Bearer access tokens before serving data.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- API KEYS VS OAUTH -->
        <section id="api-keys-vs-oauth">
          <h2>API Keys vs OAuth 2.0 Bearer Tokens</h2>
          <p>Why static API keys fail for enterprise applications compared to OAuth tokens:</p>

          <pre><code><span class="c">// 1. Static API Key Request (Insecure: static, broad, no user context)</span>
GET /api/reports HTTP/1.1
Host: api.example.com
X-API-Key: secret_live_abcdef123456789

<span class="c">// 2. OAuth 2.0 Bearer Token Request (Secure: scoped, short-lived, signed)</span>
GET /api/reports HTTP/1.1
Host: api.example.com
Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOiJSUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.eyJzdWIiOiJ1c3JfMTIzIiwic2NvcGUiOiJyZWFkOnJlcG9ydHMiLCJleHAiOjE3NTUwMDM2MDB9...</code></pre>

          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Security Dimension</th><th>Static API Key</th><th>OAuth 2.0 Bearer Token</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><strong>User Identity &amp; Context</strong></td><td>None (Application-level only)</td><td>Bound to specific user (<code>sub</code> claim)</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Token Lifetime</strong></td><td>Indefinite / Months (Hard to rotate)</td><td>Short-lived (15–60 mins) with refresh tokens</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Granular Authorization</strong></td><td>Coarse / All-or-nothing access</td><td>Fine-grained scopes (<code>read:reports</code>, <code>write:users</code>)</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Revocation Impact</strong></td><td>Catastrophic: Breaks all services sharing key</td><td>Granular: Revoke single user session without affecting others</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Cryptographic Integrity</strong></td><td>None: Shared symmetric string</td><td>Asymmetric digital signature (RS256 via public JWKS)</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </section>

        <!-- SCOPES -->
        <section id="scopes">
          <h2>OAuth Scopes &amp; User Consent</h2>
          <p>In <strong>RFC 6749 Section 3.3</strong>, <strong>Scopes</strong> are space-delimited strings defining the exact permissions an application requests. <strong>Consent</strong> is the user-facing approval step where the Resource Owner explicitly grants or denies those permissions.</p>

          <h3>1. How Scopes Translate into Consent Screens</h3>
          <p>When an application requests authorization with query parameters:</p>
          <pre><code>GET /authorize?
  response_type=code
  &amp;client_id=my_calendar_app
  &amp;redirect_uri=https%3A%2F%2Fapp.example.com%2Fcb
  &amp;scope=openid%20profile%20read:calendar%20write:calendar
  &amp;state=af0ifjsldkj HTTP/1.1
Host: accounts.example.com</code></pre>

          <div style="background:var(--surface); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:var(--radius); padding:1.5rem; margin:1.5rem 0;">
            <div style="font-weight:700; color:var(--primary); margin-bottom:0.75rem; font-size:1.1rem;">🔑 Consent Dialog (What the User Sees)</div>
            <p style="margin-bottom:0.5rem; font-size:0.95rem;"><strong>"My Calendar App"</strong> wants permission to access your account:</p>
            <ul style="margin-bottom:1rem; line-height:1.7;">
              <li>&#9989; <strong>View your basic profile:</strong> <code>openid profile</code></li>
              <li>&#9989; <strong>Read calendar events:</strong> <code>read:calendar</code></li>
              <li>&#9989; <strong>Create and modify calendar events:</strong> <code>write:calendar</code></li>
            </ul>
            <div style="display:flex; gap:0.75rem;">
              <button style="background:var(--primary); color:#000; border:none; padding:0.4rem 1rem; border-radius:4px; font-weight:600; cursor:pointer;" disabled>Allow Access</button>
              <button style="background:rgba(255,255,255,0.1); color:var(--text); border:1px solid var(--border); padding:0.4rem 1rem; border-radius:4px; cursor:pointer;" disabled>Cancel</button>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3>2. Scope Categories &amp; Examples</h3>

          <h4>A. Standard OpenID Connect (OIDC) Scopes</h4>
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Scope String</th><th>Claims Released</th><th>User Consent Description</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><code>openid</code></td><td><code>sub</code> (User identifier)</td><td>"Verify your identity"</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>profile</code></td><td><code>name</code>, <code>picture</code>, <code>locale</code></td><td>"View your basic profile information"</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>email</code></td><td><code>email</code>, <code>email_verified</code></td><td>"View your email address"</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>phone</code></td><td><code>phone_number</code></td><td>"View your phone number"</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>address</code></td><td>Formatted physical address</td><td>"View your physical address"</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>offline_access</code></td><td>Issues a Refresh Token</td><td>"Maintain access when you are not using the app"</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>

          <h4>B. Custom Microservice &amp; REST API Scopes (Action:Resource)</h4>
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Scope String</th><th>Protected Endpoint</th><th>Permission Granted</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><code>read:reports</code></td><td><code>GET /api/protected/reports</code></td><td>View internal analytical reports</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>write:reports</code></td><td><code>POST /api/protected/reports</code></td><td>Create and publish reports</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>read:users</code></td><td><code>GET /api/protected/users</code></td><td>List directory members</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>write:users</code></td><td><code>POST /api/protected/users</code></td><td>Invite and provision user accounts</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>delete:users</code></td><td><code>DELETE /api/protected/users/:id</code></td><td>Deprovision user accounts</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>execute:jobs</code></td><td><code>POST /api/protected/jobs</code></td><td>Run background asynchronous workloads</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>

          <h4>C. Real-World Cloud &amp; SaaS Scopes</h4>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Google:</strong> <code>https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive.file</code> (Manage Drive files created by app)</li>
            <li><strong>Microsoft Graph:</strong> <code>User.Read</code>, <code>Mail.Send</code>, <code>Files.ReadWrite.All</code></li>
            <li><strong>GitHub:</strong> <code>repo</code> (Full private repo control), <code>user:email</code> (Read email addresses)</li>
          </ul>

          <h3>3. Incremental (Step-Up) Consent Pattern</h3>
          <div class="callout tip">
            <strong>🛡️ Principle of Least Privilege:</strong>
            <p>Rather than demanding intrusive scopes upon initial registration, modern apps request only <code>openid profile email</code> on login, and request higher scopes (e.g. <code>write:reports</code>) <em>incrementally</em> only when the user triggers that specific feature.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- CLIENTS -->
        <section id="clients">
          <h2>Public vs Confidential Clients (RFC 6749 Section 2.1)</h2>
          <p>Clients are classified strictly by their ability to maintain the confidentiality of their credentials:</p>
          
          <div style="display:grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr)); gap:1rem; margin:1.5rem 0;">
            <div style="background:var(--surface); border:1px solid var(--border); padding:1.25rem; border-radius:var(--radius);">
              <h3 style="color:var(--primary); margin-top:0;">Confidential (Private) Clients</h3>
              <p><strong>Examples:</strong> Node.js, Express, Spring Boot, ASP.NET running in secure server environments.</p>
              <ul>
                <li>Can securely maintain a <code>client_secret</code> in server environment variables.</li>
                <li>Performs back-channel token exchanges with client authentication (Basic Auth or POST body).</li>
                <li>Eligible for Client Credentials Flow (M2M).</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
            <div style="background:var(--surface); border:1px solid var(--border); padding:1.25rem; border-radius:var(--radius);">
              <h3 style="color:var(--danger); margin-top:0;">Public Clients</h3>
              <p><strong>Examples:</strong> React/Vue Single Page Apps, iOS/Android mobile apps, desktop apps.</p>
              <ul>
                <li><strong>Cannot keep a secret:</strong> Source code is viewable via browser dev tools or binary decompilation.</li>
                <li><strong>Must NOT have a <code>client_secret</code>:</strong> Never bake a secret into an app shipped via App Store or npm bundle.</li>
                <li><strong>Must use PKCE (RFC 7636)</strong> to secure authorization code exchange.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- CLIENT TRUST -->
        <section id="client-trust">
          <h2>Client Trust &amp; Application Models</h2>
          
          <h3>1. Redirect Model vs Embedded Model</h3>
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Model</th><th>How It Works</th><th>Security Posture &amp; Best Practice</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Redirect Model (Standard)</strong></td>
                <td>Client redirects user browser to IdP login page (e.g. <code>https://auth.company.com</code>).</td>
                <td><strong>Recommended Gold Standard:</strong> Client never touches user passwords; IdP manages MFA, passkeys, and session cookies securely.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Embedded Model (Direct SDK)</strong></td>
                <td>Client renders custom input fields in its own UI and calls backend APIs (e.g. Okta Interaction Code Flow).</td>
                <td><strong>High-Trust First-Party Only:</strong> Requires high trust; client app must be strictly audited to ensure credentials are not logged or exfiltrated.</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>

          <h3>2. High-Trust First-Party vs Low-Trust Third-Party</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>First-Party Apps:</strong> Developed by the same organization hosting the identity provider (e.g., Google Maps accessing Google Account). Often granted automatic consent or native embedded login experiences.</li>
            <li><strong>Third-Party Apps:</strong> Built by external developers. <strong>Must always</strong> use the Redirect Model with explicit User Consent screens and PKCE.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <!-- CHANNELS -->
        <section id="channels">
          <h2>Front Channel vs Back Channel</h2>
          <p>OAuth 2.0 segregates interactions across two distinct network paths:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Front Channel:</strong> Carried out via user browser redirects (e.g., <code>GET /authorize</code>). Visible in browser history and logs; must only carry transient authorization codes, never raw tokens or client secrets.</li>
            <li><strong>Back Channel:</strong> Direct server-to-server TLS encrypted HTTPS POST requests (e.g., <code>POST /token</code>). Used for exchanging codes for access tokens and validating credentials.</li>
          </ul>

          <pre class="mermaid">
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    actor RO as 👤 Resource Owner (User)
    participant C as 📱 Client App (SPA / Web / Mobile)
    participant AS as 🔑 Authorization Server (IdP)
    participant RS as 🗄️ Resource Server (API)

    Note over RO,AS: 🌐 FRONT CHANNEL (Browser Redirects / Visible)
    RO-&gt;&gt;C: 1. Initiate Action / Login
    C-&gt;&gt;AS: 2. Redirect to GET /authorize (scope, state, client_id)
    RO-&gt;&gt;AS: 3. Authenticate &amp; Grant Consent
    AS--&gt;&gt;C: 4. 302 Redirect to redirect_uri with code &amp; state

    Note over C,RS: 🔒 BACK CHANNEL (Direct TLS Encrypted HTTPS POST)
    C-&gt;&gt;AS: 5. POST /token (grant_type=authorization_code, code, client_secret/PKCE)
    AS--&gt;&gt;C: 6. 200 OK (access_token, id_token, refresh_token)
    C-&gt;&gt;RS: 7. GET /api/data (Authorization: Bearer &lt;access_token&gt;)
    RS--&gt;&gt;C: 8. 200 OK (Protected JSON Payload)
          </pre>
        </section>

        <!-- FRAMEWORK VARIANCE -->
        <section id="framework-variance">
          <h2>OAuth 2.0: Framework vs Protocol Variance</h2>
          <div class="callout warning">
            <strong>⚠️ Architectural Variance &amp; Normalization:</strong>
            <p>OAuth 2.0 is defined as an <em>Authorization Framework</em> rather than a rigid protocol. Because of this, identity vendors implement parameter options, token payload formats, and role mappings differently:</p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Okta:</strong> Often issues roles inside a custom <code>groups</code> claim array: <code>"groups": ["Admins", "Engineers"]</code>.</li>
              <li><strong>Microsoft Entra ID:</strong> Issues roles inside an <code>roles</code> claim: <code>"roles": ["Admin.All", "Reports.Read"]</code>.</li>
              <li><strong>Auth0:</strong> Uses namespaced custom claims: <code>"https://myapi.com/roles": ["admin"]</code>.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>Enterprise applications and API Gateways (such as Azure APIM) must implement <strong>claim normalization adapters</strong> to map vendor-specific claims into a unified internal permission model.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ENDPOINTS -->
        <section id="endpoints">
          <h2>OAuth 2.0 Endpoints (RFC 6749, RFC 7662, RFC 7009)</h2>
          <ul>
            <li><code>/authorize</code>: Front-channel endpoint for user authentication and consent.</li>
            <li><code>/token</code>: Back-channel endpoint for code and refresh token exchanges.</li>
            <li><code>/introspect</code> (RFC 7662): Resource server endpoint to verify token status and metadata.</li>
            <li><code>/revoke</code> (RFC 7009): Endpoint for explicit client-side token revocation.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <!-- SECURITY CHECKLIST -->
        <section id="security">
          <h2>Web Security 101 Checklist (RFC 9700)</h2>
          <div style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:0.75rem; margin-top:1rem;">
            <div style="background:var(--surface); border:1px solid var(--border); padding:0.75rem 1rem; border-radius:var(--radius);">
              &#9989; <strong>Always use <code>state</code> parameter:</strong> Generates a cryptographically random nonce stored in the client session before redirecting. Prevents Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) login injection.
            </div>
            <div style="background:var(--surface); border:1px solid var(--border); padding:0.75rem 1rem; border-radius:var(--radius);">
              &#9989; <strong>Always require PKCE (RFC 7636):</strong> Mandate Proof Key for Code Exchange for all public and confidential clients to eliminate authorization code interception attacks.
            </div>
            <div style="background:var(--surface); border:1px solid var(--border); padding:0.75rem 1rem; border-radius:var(--radius);">
              &#9989; <strong>Strict Redirect URI Whitelisting:</strong> Reject wildcards and open redirectors. Validate exact scheme, domain, and path matching in the Authorization Server registration.
            </div>
            <div style="background:var(--surface); border:1px solid var(--border); padding:0.75rem 1rem; border-radius:var(--radius);">
              &#9989; <strong>Bind <code>client_id</code> to Token Requests:</strong> The Authorization Server must verify that the client redeeming an authorization code is the exact same client to which the code was issued.
            </div>
            <div style="background:var(--surface); border:1px solid var(--border); padding:0.75rem 1rem; border-radius:var(--radius);">
              &#9989; <strong>Short Token Lifetimes &amp; Refresh Rotation:</strong> Limit access tokens to 15–60 minutes. Invalidate refresh tokens on each use and revoke the entire token lineage if a reused token is detected.
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <footer class="page-footer">
          <div class="prev-next">
            <span></span>
            <a href={base + '/flows'}>Next: Grant Types &rarr;</a>
          </div>
        </footer>
      </main>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
